export const academics=[
{title:'The Work of a Seed Bank',text:`A seed bank preserves plant material so that future growers can recover varieties that have disappeared from ordinary cultivation. Its purpose differs from that of a grain warehouse: stored seeds must remain capable of producing living plants. Staff therefore test a sample from each collection before deciding how to store it.
Many crop seeds tolerate drying and low temperatures. These treatments slow the chemical reactions associated with aging, allowing seeds to remain viable for years. However, a freezer is not a permanent solution. Periodic germination tests reveal whether a collection is losing its ability to sprout. When that happens, staff grow new plants and collect a fresh generation of seeds.
Regeneration creates its own difficulty. If only a few plants reproduce, some of the original genetic diversity may be lost. Banks must grow enough individuals and prevent unwanted pollen from nearby varieties from reaching them. Detailed records identify where each sample originated and how it has been maintained.
Not every species can be conserved by this method. Some tropical seeds are damaged by drying. Living collections and other preservation techniques are needed for those plants. Seed banking is consequently one part of a broader conservation strategy, rather than a substitute for protecting plants in their natural habitats.`,qs:[Q('Why does the author distinguish a seed bank from a grain warehouse?','To emphasize that the seeds must remain able to grow','To suggest that seed banks store more food','To explain why seed banks sell their collections','To contrast the sizes of the two buildings','The first paragraph identifies the ability to produce living plants as the crucial difference.','purpose'),Q('What prompts staff to regenerate a collection?','A decline in its germination-test results','A change in the price of the crop','An increase in the number of freezers','The discovery of pollen in a warehouse','Tests reveal declining ability to sprout, after which a new generation is grown.'),Q('The word “viable” is closest in meaning to','capable of developing','easy to transport','genetically identical','widely available','The context concerns seeds remaining capable of growing.','vocabulary'),Q('Why can growing only a few plants be a problem?','It may fail to preserve the collection’s genetic variety','It makes every seed unable to tolerate cold','It requires pollen from unrelated crops','It removes the need for collection records','The passage links too few reproducing individuals to loss of diversity.','inference'),Q('Which statement best summarizes the passage?','Seed banks preserve living potential through careful storage and renewal, but cannot conserve every plant','Freezing all seeds eliminates the need for other conservation methods','Seed banks mainly distribute grain when farms produce too little','Tropical seeds survive longer than crop seeds under identical conditions','Storage, regeneration and limitations organize the entire passage.','main idea')]},
{title:'Reading a River’s Past',text:`A river seldom follows one unchanging path across a plain. Over decades, bends migrate as water removes sediment from one bank and deposits it on another. Eventually a river may abandon a bend entirely. Although vegetation can hide the old channel, traces of it remain in the landscape.
Researchers combine several kinds of evidence to reconstruct these earlier routes. Aerial photographs may reveal curved lines in fields because plants grow differently above buried sand and clay. Historical maps offer another view, but their accuracy varies. A map produced to define property boundaries may represent a channel more carefully than one drawn primarily to show roads.
Sediment cores provide a way to investigate features that photographs only suggest. A core is a narrow column of material extracted from the ground. Coarse sand at one depth and fine mud above it can indicate a change from flowing water to a quieter environment. Dating suitable material helps establish when that change occurred.
No single clue determines the whole story. A curved mark could have another origin, and a core represents only a small location. Agreement among photographs, maps and several cores makes an interpretation stronger. Reconstructing past channels is useful beyond historical curiosity: former riverbeds can influence where floodwater moves and where buildings encounter unstable ground.`,qs:[Q('What is the passage mainly about?','Combining evidence to identify former river channels','Designing roads to prevent rivers from moving','Comparing modern rivers with ocean currents','Explaining why all old maps are unreliable','The passage describes complementary methods and their applications.','main idea'),Q('Why might plants reveal a buried river channel?','Different buried materials affect their growth','Plants preserve the exact date of a flood','All plants grow only on coarse sand','Roots reproduce the markings of old maps','Different growth above sand and clay can reveal curved traces.'),Q('Why does the author discuss a map’s original purpose?','Its purpose may affect which details were recorded accurately','Its purpose determines the age of sediment','Property maps always omit rivers','Road maps are necessarily older than property maps','The property-boundary example explains differences in accuracy.','purpose'),Q('What does a layer of mud above coarse sand potentially indicate?','A shift to less energetic water conditions','A river that has never changed direction','A recent increase in the speed of the current','The absence of water throughout the site’s history','Coarse material is associated with flow, finer mud with quieter conditions.','inference'),Q('The word “reconstruct” is closest in meaning to','work out from surviving evidence','physically rebuild in the same place','copy without making changes','remove from the landscape','Researchers infer earlier routes from evidence rather than rebuild the river.','vocabulary')]},
{title:'Why Museums Rest Some Objects',text:`Visitors sometimes assume that an object absent from a museum gallery has been judged unimportant. In fact, rotating objects can be essential to their preservation. Light provides a clear example. It allows visitors to see an object, but exposure can also change the materials from which the object is made.
Colored textiles and works on paper are particularly vulnerable. Light can break chemical bonds in dyes, gradually changing their appearance. The effect depends on both brightness and duration. A moderate light level sustained for many months may cause more damage than a brief period of stronger illumination. Conservators therefore consider cumulative exposure rather than brightness alone.
Putting an object in darkness does not reverse fading that has already happened. A rest period instead limits the additional damage it receives. Museums may display one textile for a short season and replace it with another while keeping environmental conditions carefully controlled. Digital reproductions sometimes remain available when the original is in storage.
Rotation also creates a communication problem. A visitor who has traveled to see a famous piece may be disappointed. Explaining the conservation schedule helps visitors understand the decision. The museum must balance access now against the possibility of access in the future. Displaying everything continuously would maximize immediate visibility while potentially diminishing what future visitors could experience.`,qs:[Q('What is the main reason for rotating vulnerable objects out of galleries?','To reduce the damage caused by continued exposure','To restore colors automatically in dark storage','To increase the market price of every object','To show that textiles are less important than paintings','Rotation limits additional exposure and damage.'),Q('What does “cumulative” emphasize in paragraph 2?','The total effect of exposure over time','The original cost of the lighting equipment','The difference between visitors’ preferences','The number of chemicals used in restoration','Brightness and duration together determine cumulative exposure.','vocabulary'),Q('What can be inferred about a faded textile placed in darkness?','Its existing fading will remain even though further exposure is limited','Its original color will return after a short rest','It will become safe for unlimited later display','It will no longer need environmental controls','The text explicitly distinguishes prevention from reversal.','inference'),Q('Why are digital reproductions mentioned?','They can preserve some visitor access when an original is not displayed','They remove all reasons to preserve originals','They provide a method of chemically repairing dyes','They help identify the makers of anonymous objects','Reproductions remain available during storage.','purpose'),Q('Which tension structures the final paragraph?','Present access versus long-term preservation','Visitor numbers versus ticket prices','Historical accuracy versus artistic creativity','Lighting costs versus staffing costs','The final paragraph contrasts current visibility with future access.','main idea')]},
{title:'Learning from an Unsuccessful Prediction',text:`Scientific models are simplified accounts of how a system behaves. A model might predict when a lake will develop an algal bloom using measurements of temperature and nutrients. If a bloom occurs earlier than predicted, the discrepancy does not by itself show that modeling is useless. It can instead identify something important that the model leaves out.
Researchers first examine the observations. A faulty instrument or an irregular sampling schedule may produce an apparent mismatch. If the measurements are dependable, they investigate the assumptions connecting those measurements to the predicted event. For example, mixing caused by wind might bring nutrients to the surface even when average nutrient measurements remain low.
Adding more variables is not automatically an improvement. A model can become so closely fitted to one set of observations that it performs poorly on new data. Researchers therefore reserve independent observations for testing. A useful revision should explain the initial failure while retaining its ability to make predictions beyond that particular case.
This process gives unsuccessful predictions a productive role. They help distinguish incomplete explanations from failures in measurement, and they suggest new observations to collect. A model earns confidence through repeated testing across circumstances, not simply through the number of details it contains or its success in describing events that are already known.`,qs:[Q('What is the author’s main point?','A failed prediction can guide useful improvements in a scientific model','Scientific models should contain every observable variable','Models of lakes cannot produce useful predictions','Accurate instruments make testing models unnecessary','The passage presents failure as evidence for investigation and revision.','main idea'),Q('What should researchers check before revising the model’s assumptions?','Whether the observations themselves are reliable','Whether every variable can be removed','Whether the model has a complex name','Whether earlier predictions can be kept secret','The second paragraph first examines measurement and sampling.'),Q('Why does the author mention wind?','To illustrate a potentially omitted influence','To identify the only cause of algal blooms','To argue that nutrient measurements should stop','To explain how to repair a faulty thermometer','Wind mixing illustrates an omitted process.','purpose'),Q('What problem can arise when a model is fitted too closely to one dataset?','It may predict new observations poorly','It will contain too few variables to describe the old data','It will prevent any instrument from making measurements','It will necessarily predict blooms later in every lake','The third paragraph describes overfitting without requiring that term.','inference'),Q('In this passage, “discrepancy” means','a difference between expected and observed outcomes','an agreement among competing explanations','a standard method of collecting samples','a decrease in the number of measurements','The earlier-than-predicted bloom creates the discrepancy.','vocabulary')]},
{title:'Urban Trees and Unequal Cooling',text:`Trees can make a city street more comfortable on a hot day. Their shade reduces the solar energy reaching pavement, while evaporation from leaves transfers some heat into water vapor. These mechanisms help explain why a tree-lined block may feel different from a nearby street with little vegetation.
However, simply counting trees is an incomplete measure of the cooling people experience. A young tree with a narrow crown may offer little shade, whereas a mature tree can cover a large pedestrian area. Placement also matters. Trees in an inaccessible courtyard provide different benefits from those shading a busy bus stop. Measures of canopy cover and the location of shade therefore complement tree counts.
Maintenance introduces another complication. Trees need suitable soil, water and protection from damage. Planting many seedlings without supporting their survival may produce impressive initial statistics but little lasting cooling. Some neighborhoods face greater barriers to maintenance, including limited space beneath sidewalks and fewer resources for watering.
Urban planning consequently involves more than choosing a numerical planting target. Residents can identify routes and gathering places where shade is most needed, while arborists assess which species can survive local conditions. A program that considers survival, canopy growth and everyday access is more likely to distribute benefits fairly than one evaluated only by the number of planting events.`,qs:[Q('Which statement best captures the passage’s argument?','Cooling benefits depend on tree size, location and survival as well as quantity','All urban tree species provide identical cooling','Counting seedlings is the most complete measure of success','Trees cool streets only by preventing wind','The passage expands evaluation beyond simple counts.','main idea'),Q('How do leaves help cool the surrounding environment?','Through evaporation that transfers heat into water vapor','By storing sunlight permanently inside their branches','By preventing all water from entering the soil','Through a process that makes nearby pavement absorb more sunlight','Evaporation is the second cooling mechanism identified.'),Q('Why does the author contrast a courtyard with a bus stop?','To show that the usefulness of shade depends on where it is accessible','To suggest that courtyards should never contain trees','To compare the costs of two transportation systems','To establish that trees grow faster beside roads','The contrast concerns everyday access to shade.','purpose'),Q('What can be inferred about a program that reports only initial planting numbers?','It may overstate the lasting benefits of its work','It must have selected the most suitable species','It has necessarily distributed cooling fairly','It has already measured mature canopy cover','Seedlings that do not survive contribute little lasting cooling.','inference'),Q('“Complement” in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to','add useful information to','completely replace','make less reliable','prevent the collection of','Canopy measurements add to, rather than eliminate, counts.','vocabulary')]},
{title:'The Economics of Repair',text:`When a household appliance stops working, replacing it may appear simpler than repairing it. Yet the decision depends on more than the price of a replacement. Repair involves diagnosis, skilled labor and access to compatible parts. If one of these is difficult to obtain, even a minor fault can make an otherwise serviceable product expensive to fix.
Design influences this calculation. A casing held together with accessible screws can be opened without destroying it. A sealed assembly may require replacing several working components along with the failed one. Manufacturers also influence repair through the availability of manuals and spare parts. The same technical failure can therefore have different economic consequences in differently designed products.
Community repair events address some of these barriers. Volunteers may help diagnose faults or demonstrate a simple procedure. Such events can reduce uncertainty and increase practical knowledge, but they cannot guarantee that every item is safe or economical to repair. Electrical hazards and unavailable components still impose limits.
The wider significance of repair includes the materials and energy used to manufacture replacements. Keeping an appliance in use can postpone those demands. Nevertheless, an unusually inefficient old appliance may create a different trade-off. Evaluating repair requires attention to the particular product and its continued operation, rather than assuming that either repair or replacement is always the better choice.`,qs:[Q('What is the passage mainly concerned with?','The interacting factors that affect whether repair is worthwhile','A claim that all old appliances should be replaced immediately','Instructions for opening a particular appliance safely','The historical origin of community volunteer organizations','The passage considers price, design, knowledge and environmental trade-offs.','main idea'),Q('Why can a small fault make an appliance costly to fix?','Diagnosis or compatible parts may be difficult to obtain','Every small fault consumes more electricity','Repair events always charge high admission fees','New products cannot contain replaceable parts','Access to diagnosis, labor and parts determines repair cost.'),Q('What does the comparison of screws and sealed assemblies illustrate?','Product design can affect the cost and feasibility of repair','Screws make all appliances unsafe to use','Sealed products always use less manufacturing energy','Repair skills are unnecessary when manuals exist','The comparison links design to economic consequences.','purpose'),Q('What limitation of community repair events is mentioned?','They cannot overcome every safety or parts problem','They accept only newly manufactured appliances','They necessarily increase uncertainty about faults','They prevent participants from acquiring skills','The third paragraph explicitly retains hazards and parts limitations.'),Q('What can be inferred from the example of an inefficient old appliance?','Continued operation can complicate the environmental case for repair','Manufacturing materials should never enter the calculation','Replacement is environmentally superior in every situation','Energy efficiency has no relation to repair decisions','Operating impacts can offset some benefits of avoiding replacement.','inference')]},
{title:'How a Market Becomes a Meeting Place',text:`A weekly market is a place to exchange goods, but its social role may extend beyond transactions. People who arrive at similar times repeatedly encounter the same sellers and customers. Brief conversations about products can develop into recognition and, eventually, relationships that persist outside the market itself.
The physical arrangement of the space influences these encounters. Benches near food stalls allow visitors to remain after shopping. Narrow passages may increase contact, but overcrowding can discourage people who find the space uncomfortable. A successful social space does not simply force the greatest number of people into the smallest area.
Researchers studying markets therefore observe how people use them rather than relying only on attendance totals. A large crowd might consist mainly of customers who leave quickly. A smaller market could support longer visits and more repeated interactions. Interviews can reveal whether participants feel welcome, while observation can show which groups actually use particular areas.
Markets are not automatically inclusive. High prices, limited opening hours or inaccessible entrances may exclude some residents. Organizers who want a market to function as a neighborhood meeting place must consider these barriers alongside commercial success. The market’s social contribution emerges from patterns of participation, and those patterns depend partly on deliberate decisions about space, access and scheduling.`,qs:[Q('What is the passage’s central claim?','A market’s social value depends on how people participate and how access is organized','Attendance totals alone reveal a market’s social success','All commercial transactions create lasting friendships','The most crowded market is necessarily the most inclusive','The argument combines repeated interaction, design and barriers.','main idea'),Q('How might benches contribute to social interaction?','They encourage visitors to stay after making purchases','They remove the need for accessible entrances','They ensure that product prices become lower','They prevent visitors from talking to sellers','Remaining in the space creates further opportunities for encounters.'),Q('Why are large and small markets compared?','To show why crowd size alone is an inadequate measure','To establish that small markets always sell better products','To explain why interviews should replace observation','To show that all visitors prefer narrow passages','Duration and recurrence of participation matter in addition to numbers.','purpose'),Q('Which condition could restrict participation?','Opening hours that some residents cannot use','The availability of places to sit','Repeated contact with familiar sellers','Observation of how visitors use the space','Limited hours are explicitly named as a barrier.'),Q('The word “deliberate” is closest in meaning to','planned and intentional','temporary and accidental','expensive and unnecessary','slow and uncertain','The decisions are actively made by organizers.','vocabulary')]},
{title:'Signals in an Ice Core',text:`Ice accumulating in a cold region can preserve evidence of past environments. Snow becomes compressed beneath later snowfall, and small bubbles of air may eventually be trapped within the ice. A core drilled through the accumulated layers offers a sequence of samples rather than a single snapshot of the past.
Different features answer different questions. Air bubbles can preserve samples of an earlier atmosphere. Particles such as dust may reflect changes in winds or exposed land surfaces. Certain chemical properties of the ice provide clues about the conditions under which the original snow formed. Interpreting these clues requires understanding how each signal entered the ice.
The depth of a sample is related to its age, but the relationship is not necessarily uniform. Layers become compressed, snowfall rates change and flowing ice can deform older material. Researchers combine layer counting, known events and physical models to build an age estimate. They also report uncertainty rather than assigning every depth a perfectly precise date.
A further distinction concerns the age of the trapped air. Air can continue moving through porous snow before the surrounding material becomes sealed ice. Consequently, the air in a bubble may be younger than the ice enclosing it. Careful interpretation therefore involves comparing several records and their dating assumptions, not treating every signal at the same depth as exactly simultaneous.`,qs:[Q('What is the main purpose of the passage?','To explain both the value and interpretive challenges of ice-core evidence','To argue that ice contains no useful atmospheric information','To describe a method for making artificial ice','To establish that all ice layers accumulate at an identical rate','The passage explains signals and the limits of their chronology.','main idea'),Q('What can trapped air bubbles provide?','Samples of an earlier atmosphere','The exact age of every dust particle','A record unaffected by the formation of ice','Direct measurements of future snowfall','The second paragraph identifies preserved atmospheric samples.'),Q('Why is the depth–age relationship not always uniform?','Accumulation, compression and deformation can vary','Every annual layer has the same thickness','Researchers always drill only shallow cores','Air bubbles prevent snow from accumulating','The third paragraph lists varying physical processes.'),Q('What can be inferred about two signals at the same depth?','They may represent different times','They necessarily record the same event','They must have entered the ice through identical processes','They cannot be compared with any other record','Air can remain mobile until after the enclosing snow was deposited.','inference'),Q('“Porous” most nearly describes material that','contains spaces through which air can move','has no relationship to snowfall','is completely sealed against gases','is always warmer than liquid water','Continued air movement through snow explains the contextual meaning.','vocabulary')]},
{title:'When Birds Change Their Routes',text:`Migrating birds do not always use exactly the same route each year. Weather, food availability and the condition of stopping places can alter the costs of a journey. A route that is geographically shorter may require crossing a region where a bird cannot easily rest or feed.
Stopover sites therefore form an important part of migration. At these locations, birds replenish energy before continuing. Researchers can investigate their importance by tracking individuals and measuring how long they remain. A long stay, however, has more than one possible interpretation. It might indicate abundant food and a useful resting place, or poor conditions that prevent an exhausted bird from leaving.
Combining movement records with observations of feeding and local weather helps distinguish these explanations. Researchers also compare several individuals rather than assuming that one bird represents an entire population. Age and previous experience may affect the decisions an individual makes.
These findings have consequences for conservation. Protecting breeding and wintering areas may be insufficient if birds lose essential places between them. A network of usable sites can provide alternatives when conditions change. This does not mean that every route variation is harmless. Instead, it suggests that conservation should consider the flexibility birds need throughout a journey, as well as the endpoints usually drawn on a migration map.`,qs:[Q('Why might a bird avoid the shortest route?','It may offer few opportunities to rest and feed','Birds are unable to detect weather changes','Longer routes always contain fewer predators','Geographic distance has no energetic cost','The passage distinguishes distance from the conditions encountered.'),Q('What makes the length of a stopover difficult to interpret by itself?','Both favorable and unfavorable conditions can produce a long stay','Tracking devices cannot record time','Every bird stays for the same number of days','Food availability is unrelated to departure','The second paragraph offers two opposing explanations.','inference'),Q('Why do researchers compare several birds?','Individual experience and age may influence decisions','One bird always takes every possible route','All birds share identical levels of experience','Local weather can be measured only by several devices','The passage cautions against treating one individual as a population.','purpose'),Q('What conservation approach does the passage support?','Protecting usable stopping places as well as journey endpoints','Protecting wintering areas while removing stopover sites','Keeping every bird on one compulsory route','Ignoring changes in routes when populations decline','The final paragraph recommends a network along the journey.','main idea'),Q('“Replenish” is closest in meaning to','restore a used supply','measure a fixed amount','use up completely','transfer to another bird','Birds restore energy through feeding at stopovers.','vocabulary')]},
{title:'The Hidden Labor of a Catalogue',text:`A library catalogue can make finding a book seem almost effortless. Behind a short search result, however, lies a set of decisions about how the item should be described. A title alone is often insufficient: different books can share a title, and the same work can appear in several editions or languages.
Cataloguers identify authors, dates and relationships among versions. They also assign subject terms so that a reader need not already know the exact title. Consistency matters. If related books are described using unrelated terms, a single search may fail to retrieve them together. Shared standards allow records created by different institutions to be exchanged.
Standardization is useful but not neutral in all its effects. A historical subject label may no longer match the terms used by the people being described. Changing it can improve access and respect, but the change must be handled carefully so that older records remain discoverable. Some catalogues link alternative terms rather than simply erasing earlier descriptions.
Digital searching has not eliminated these issues. An automated search can rapidly process a large collection, but the results still depend on the information attached to each item. The apparent simplicity of a search interface consequently rests on continuing human work: identifying distinctions, maintaining connections and revising descriptions as collections and users change.`,qs:[Q('What does the passage primarily explain?','Why simple catalogue searches depend on continuing descriptive work','Why libraries should organize books only by title','How digital searching has removed the need for standards','Why all historical catalogue terms should remain unchanged','The passage follows the decisions underlying the search interface.','main idea'),Q('Why is a title not always sufficient to identify an item?','Different books and versions can have the same title','Titles cannot be entered into digital catalogues','All books in one library have different subjects','A title necessarily identifies the author’s language','The first paragraph explains ambiguity among works and versions.'),Q('What is one purpose of shared cataloguing standards?','Allowing institutions to exchange usable records','Preventing libraries from adding new books','Making every collection contain identical editions','Eliminating the need for subject information','The second paragraph explicitly connects standards with exchange.'),Q('Why might a catalogue link alternative subject terms?','To improve current access while keeping older records discoverable','To make searches return fewer related materials','To ensure that labels never change in meaning','To hide the subjects of historical books','Linking terms balances revision with continuity.','inference'),Q('The word “neutral” is closest in meaning to','free from effects favoring particular perspectives','difficult for computers to store','unrelated to any historical period','identical across all languages','The discussion concerns the effects of descriptive terminology.','vocabulary')]},
{title:'A Building That Can Be Taken Apart',text:`Most discussions of sustainable buildings focus on their operation: heating, cooling and lighting. Another approach asks what will happen when a building is no longer needed. Designing for disassembly means planning how components can eventually be separated and used again, rather than treating demolition as an unavoidable final stage.
Connections are central to this approach. Beams joined with removable bolts are easier to separate than elements permanently bonded together. Materials also need to be identifiable. A component whose composition and loading history are documented is easier to evaluate for reuse than an apparently similar object of uncertain origin.
There are limits to the strategy. Removable connections must still meet safety requirements during decades of use. Designers cannot assume that future builders will want exactly the same dimensions or materials. Storage, transport and testing may also make reuse costly. A theoretical possibility of recovery is not the same as an established market for recovered parts.
Nevertheless, planning for a later transition can change decisions made at the beginning. Designers may choose adaptable spaces that remain useful as needs change, delaying disassembly altogether. The broader aim is to preserve the value of materials and structures for as long as practical. Recovering an individual beam is one route toward that aim, but extending the useful life of an entire building can be another.`,qs:[Q('What distinguishes designing for disassembly?','Planning from the outset for components to be recovered','Reducing safety standards when a building is old','Using only materials that require no connections','Treating demolition as the only possible final stage','The initial design anticipates later separation and reuse.','main idea'),Q('Why are removable bolts mentioned?','They illustrate a connection that facilitates later separation','They are the only legally permitted connection','They make loading-history records unnecessary','They guarantee a buyer for every recovered beam','Bolts provide a concrete example of disassembly-friendly design.','purpose'),Q('What makes a documented component easier to reuse?','Its material and previous use can be evaluated','It never needs safety testing','It can fit every future building design','It can always be transported without cost','Documentation reduces uncertainty about composition and history.'),Q('What does the author imply about the market for recovered parts?','It cannot be assumed merely because parts can be separated','It already accepts all recovered materials at a fixed price','It is unaffected by testing and storage costs','It prevents buildings from being adapted','Recoverability and an actual market are distinguished.','inference'),Q('What relationship does the final paragraph establish?','Adaptation and disassembly can serve the same wider objective','Adaptation necessarily makes reuse impossible','Recovering a beam is always better than retaining a building','A building’s useful life has no connection to sustainability','Both approaches can preserve material value.','organization')]},
{title:'Why a Familiar Tune Seems Easier',text:`A melody heard repeatedly can become easier to recognize even when listeners are not deliberately trying to learn it. Repeated exposure allows them to form expectations about which sounds will come next. Recognition then involves more than recalling isolated notes: listeners anticipate patterns across time.
Researchers distinguish familiarity from musical expertise. A person with little formal training may recognize a song heard often, while a trained musician may struggle with an unfamiliar musical tradition. Training can provide strategies for analyzing rhythm or harmony, but familiarity supplies knowledge of particular patterns and conventions.
Experiments must account for this distinction. If one group performs better on a listening task, the difference may reflect previous exposure rather than a general advantage in perception. Researchers can ask participants about their listening habits or use newly composed melodies that neither group has heard. Neither solution is perfect. Self-reports can be incomplete, and a new melody can still resemble a familiar style.
The distinction also helps explain why unfamiliar music can become more accessible over time. Listeners gradually learn which events are expected and which are surprising. This does not mean that repeated listening guarantees enjoyment. Recognition, prediction and preference are related processes, but a melody can be entirely familiar without becoming a favorite.`,qs:[Q('What is the main subject?','How familiarity and training influence musical recognition','Why formal training guarantees enjoyment of every melody','How to compose a melody without rhythm','Why repeated exposure prevents musical learning','Recognition is examined through exposure, expertise and experimental design.','main idea'),Q('Repeated exposure helps listeners primarily by allowing them to','develop expectations about upcoming sounds','remember only the first isolated note','ignore all differences in musical style','replace hearing with visual information','The first paragraph connects exposure with predictive patterns.'),Q('Why might a trained musician struggle with an unfamiliar tradition?','Training does not provide familiarity with every set of conventions','Musical training eliminates sensitivity to rhythm','Expert musicians never listen outside their training','Recognition requires an identical instrument in every performance','Training and culture-specific familiarity are distinguished.','inference'),Q('Why are newly composed melodies useful in experiments?','They reduce differences caused by hearing the exact melody before','They guarantee that no familiar stylistic patterns appear','They remove the need to compare participants','They measure preference without involving recognition','Novel melodies address prior exposure, though not perfectly.','purpose'),Q('Which distinction is made in the final paragraph?','Recognizing a melody does not necessarily mean liking it','Prediction requires no learning','Unfamiliar music can never become accessible','Musical preference is identical for all trained listeners','The paragraph separates familiarity from enjoyment.','detail')]},
{title:'The Problem with an Average',text:`An average can summarize a large collection of measurements in a convenient number. If a town reports its average household water use, residents can compare their own use with a general pattern. Yet the summary does not reveal how much households differ from one another.
Consider two streets with the same average consumption. On one street, nearly every household uses a similar amount. On the other, several households use very little while a few use extremely large amounts. A campaign based on the average alone might treat both streets alike even though the opportunities for reducing consumption differ.
Researchers therefore examine the distribution of measurements as well as a central value. They may report the median, the value in the middle of an ordered list, or describe how widely observations are spread. These measures answer different questions. The median is less influenced by a small number of extreme observations, but it does not make the extremes irrelevant to planning.
The most useful summary depends on the decision being made. A water supplier estimating total demand may need information that differs from what a household needs when comparing itself with similar neighbors. Averages are not inherently misleading; problems arise when a convenient summary is asked to answer a question it was not designed to address.`,qs:[Q('What is the author’s principal point?','A statistical summary should be chosen for the question being asked','Averages should never be used in public planning','The median always provides all necessary information','Household water use cannot be measured accurately','The final paragraph explicitly ties usefulness to the decision.','main idea'),Q('What do the two streets demonstrate?','Identical averages can conceal different patterns of use','Every household uses the average amount','Extreme observations always cancel each other','Water-saving opportunities depend only on street length','The distributions differ even though the averages agree.','purpose'),Q('How is the median defined?','The middle value in an ordered set','The sum of all measurements','The largest observed measurement','The difference between the two largest values','The third paragraph supplies this definition.'),Q('What does the passage imply about unusually large users?','They may still matter even if the median is used','They become irrelevant once identified','They necessarily represent measurement errors','They must occur on every street','Resistance of the median to extremes does not eliminate their planning relevance.','inference'),Q('“Distribution” refers here to','the pattern of values across the measurements','the delivery of water to individual buildings','the publication of a town’s annual report','the replacement of faulty measuring equipment','The context concerns variability and the spread of observations.','vocabulary')]},
{title:'Restoring a Meadow',text:`Restoring a damaged meadow involves more than scattering seeds. Plants interact with the soil, available light and neighboring species, so the same seed mixture may succeed at one location and fail at another. Restoration teams begin by investigating why the original plant community declined.
In some meadows, nutrients added during earlier agricultural use favor rapidly growing grasses. Those grasses can shade out smaller flowering plants. Adding more flower seeds does little if the seedlings cannot obtain enough light. Managers may remove cut vegetation after mowing so that nutrients are gradually carried away rather than returned to the soil as the material decomposes.
Timing is also important. Cutting a meadow too early can prevent desirable plants from producing seeds. Cutting too late may allow aggressive species to dominate. A schedule must reflect local conditions and the life cycles of the plants that managers hope to encourage.
Progress is usually uneven. Some species establish quickly, while others arrive slowly from nearby populations or require conditions that take years to develop. Monitoring helps managers distinguish a temporary delay from a persistent obstacle. Restoration is therefore an ongoing process of observing and adjusting, not a single intervention with an immediately visible final result. A visually green field may still be far from the diverse meadow that a project intends to recover.`,qs:[Q('Why might adding flower seeds fail in a nutrient-rich meadow?','Fast-growing grasses can deprive seedlings of light','All flowering plants need permanently shaded conditions','Nutrients prevent every plant from producing seeds','The seeds necessarily come from nearby populations','Grasses benefit from nutrients and shade out smaller plants.'),Q('Why remove vegetation after mowing?','To carry nutrients out of the meadow','To return more nutrients through decomposition','To stop all natural seed dispersal','To make every species establish at the same rate','Removing the cut material reduces nutrient return.','purpose'),Q('What determines an appropriate mowing schedule?','Local conditions and the life cycles of desirable plants','The number of visitors to the meadow alone','A single date suitable for every meadow','The appearance of green vegetation regardless of species','Timing must accommodate establishment, reproduction and competition.'),Q('What can be inferred about a green field?','Its appearance alone does not establish restoration success','It necessarily contains the intended diversity','It no longer requires monitoring','It has recovered all the original species','Green cover can conceal an undesirable species composition.','inference'),Q('What is the passage mainly explaining?','Why meadow restoration requires diagnosis and continuing adjustment','Why seed mixtures should never be used','Why nutrient addition always improves biodiversity','How to replace all meadow plants in a single season','The entire text develops an adaptive management process.','main idea')]},
{title:'Borrowing as a Social Arrangement',text:`A tool library lends equipment that people may need only occasionally. Instead of buying a drill for a single project, a member can borrow one and return it for someone else to use. This arrangement can reduce the number of products that must be owned individually, but its success depends on more than having shelves full of tools.
Demand is uneven. Many members may want gardening equipment on the same weekend, while another item remains unused for months. Keeping records of loans helps organizers decide which tools to buy in greater numbers and which purchases would add little value. A rarely borrowed tool is not necessarily useless, however; it may meet an unusual need that would otherwise be costly to satisfy.
Trust and responsibility matter as well. Members need clear instructions about safe use, cleaning and reporting faults. If damaged tools return to the shelf without inspection, the next borrower may face inconvenience or danger. Maintenance work is therefore part of the lending service rather than an optional extra.
Tool libraries also provide opportunities for people to exchange practical knowledge. A demonstration can help a borrower use unfamiliar equipment confidently. The library is consequently both a collection of objects and a system of relationships, information and ongoing care that makes shared access possible.`,qs:[Q('What is the passage’s main claim?','Successful tool lending requires organization and care as well as equipment','A large collection automatically meets every community need','Members should buy every item they borrow','Tool libraries have no function beyond storing objects','Demand, trust, maintenance and knowledge support shared access.','main idea'),Q('How can loan records help organizers?','They show patterns useful for purchasing decisions','They guarantee that every tool will be used equally','They eliminate the need for safety instructions','They prove that rarely borrowed tools have no value','Usage records inform what to duplicate or add.'),Q('Why does the author caution against dismissing a rarely borrowed tool?','It might serve an uncommon but important need','It always costs less to maintain than popular tools','It is necessarily the newest tool in the collection','It can replace every commonly used item','Rare use does not necessarily imply low value.','purpose'),Q('What problem can result from failing to inspect returned equipment?','A later borrower may receive an unsafe tool','Demand will become evenly distributed','Borrowers will no longer need demonstrations','All loan records will become inaccurate','Fault reporting and inspection protect later users.'),Q('The final paragraph broadens the discussion by considering','the exchange of knowledge alongside the lending of objects','a reason to end all equipment lending','the history of manufacturing garden tools','the replacement of membership with retail sales','The service includes relationships and information.','organization')]},
{title:'A Landscape Shaped by Wind',text:`Sand dunes can appear permanent when viewed during a brief visit, yet they are continually shaped by movement. Wind lifts or pushes grains along the surface, and grains may accumulate where the moving air loses energy. A dune’s form reflects both this transport and the obstacles the wind encounters.
Vegetation can alter the process. Stems and leaves slow air near the ground, encouraging sand to settle. Roots help hold the accumulated material in place. The resulting pattern is not simply a case of plants stopping all movement. Some dune plants can grow upward as sand gathers around them, gradually influencing the shape of the dune.
Human activity can interrupt these interactions. Repeated footsteps may damage plants and create a bare path through which sand moves more readily. A small gap can widen as wind carries additional material through it. Boardwalks are sometimes installed to concentrate visitors on a durable route while reducing damage to neighboring vegetation.
Management must still account for the ecological role of movement. Completely stabilizing every dune would not necessarily protect all the organisms that depend on these environments. Some species need open, shifting sand. Understanding dunes therefore requires attention to both persistence and change, and conservation decisions must consider which processes support the particular habitat being managed.`,qs:[Q('What primarily shapes the formation of dunes?','The movement and deposition of sand by wind','The complete absence of moving air','The growth of roots without any transported material','A single event that fixes the landscape permanently','Transport and deposition are the central processes.'),Q('How do plants encourage sand to accumulate?','They slow near-ground air so that grains settle','They make all nearby wind stronger','They convert roots directly into sand','They prevent every grain from moving anywhere','The second paragraph links reduced air speed with settling.'),Q('Why are boardwalks mentioned?','As a way to limit vegetation damage caused by visitors','As proof that dunes need no visitor management','As structures that eliminate all sand movement','As a method for replacing native vegetation','Boardwalks concentrate foot traffic and protect adjacent plants.','purpose'),Q('Why might total dune stabilization be undesirable?','Some organisms require open and moving sand','Every plant dies on stable surfaces','Stable dunes can never support any wildlife','Conservation depends only on the number of visitors','The final paragraph identifies species dependent on shifting habitat.','inference'),Q('The passage emphasizes that dune conservation should consider','the processes maintaining a particular habitat','only whether the landscape looks unchanged','the elimination of all natural disturbance','identical management for every dune system','The conclusion rejects a one-size-fits-all preference for stability.','main idea')]},
{title:'The Value of a Negative Result',text:`An experiment that finds no effect can be difficult to publish. Researchers and readers may be more interested in a new treatment that produces an improvement than in one that appears to do nothing. Yet overlooking negative results can distort the evidence available for making decisions.
Suppose several teams test the same treatment. If only the successful experiments become widely visible, a later reviewer may conclude that the treatment is more dependable than the full set of experiments would suggest. The problem is not necessarily dishonesty within any individual report. It can arise from the selective visibility of findings across many reports.
Publishing every unsuccessful experiment without evaluation is not a solution. A study may be too small to detect an important effect or may use an unsuitable measure. Readers need enough methodological information to interpret what the absence of a detected effect actually means. A well-designed negative study can rule out some possibilities, while a weak one may leave the question open.
Some research systems address selective reporting by recording study plans before results are known. Such records make it easier to identify completed studies that were never published. The aim is not to treat all findings as equally informative, but to prevent the direction of a result from being the main reason it enters the shared body of evidence.`,qs:[Q('What is the passage’s central concern?','Selective reporting can distort the overall evidence about a treatment','Every negative result proves that a treatment is useless','Unpublished research always involves dishonest measurements','Only successful experiments need methodological descriptions','The text examines how visibility affects the combined evidence.','main idea'),Q('Why might a reviewer overestimate a treatment’s dependability?','Unsuccessful experiments may be missing from the visible record','All teams necessarily tested different treatments','Negative studies always use larger samples','A reviewer cannot read methodological information','The second paragraph explains a biased visible sample of studies.'),Q('What qualification does paragraph 3 introduce?','Negative results differ in how informative they are','Negative studies should never be published','Small studies always exclude every possible effect','A missing effect requires no interpretation','Study size and measurement influence the meaning of a negative result.','organization'),Q('What is the purpose of recording study plans in advance?','To help identify studies absent from the published record','To guarantee that every treatment will succeed','To prevent researchers from measuring any outcomes','To remove the need for later review','Registration makes missing completed studies easier to locate.','purpose'),Q('“Rule out” is closest in meaning to','eliminate as a reasonable possibility','include without testing','describe as highly desirable','publish earlier than expected','A strong negative study can exclude some explanations.','vocabulary')]},
{title:'Why Some Instructions Use Pictures',text:`Instructions for assembling an object often combine words with diagrams. A diagram can show the relative positions of parts more directly than a long verbal description. This does not mean that pictures are universally easier to understand. Their usefulness depends on what information the task requires and what the reader can interpret.
An unfamiliar symbol can create uncertainty. A curved arrow might mean that a component should rotate, but a reader may not know whether the rotation applies to the whole assembly or one small part. Designers can reduce ambiguity by showing an intermediate state or labeling the relevant component.
Testing instructions with users frequently reveals difficulties that experts overlook. Someone who already knows how an object fits together may mentally supply a missing step. A novice cannot necessarily make the same inference. Asking users to explain what they intend to do before they act can expose a misunderstanding without waiting for the object to be assembled incorrectly.
Effective instructions therefore use representations strategically. Words may be better for stating a condition or warning, while diagrams clarify spatial relationships. The goal is not to minimize the number of words at any cost. It is to coordinate the available forms of information so that a reader can carry out the required action accurately and safely.`,qs:[Q('What does the passage argue about pictures in instructions?','They are useful for some information but must be designed and tested carefully','They always make written explanations unnecessary','Their meaning is identical for every reader','They are useful only after an assembly has failed','The text balances spatial benefits against ambiguity and user needs.','main idea'),Q('What potential problem does the curved-arrow example illustrate?','A symbol may not clearly identify which part should move','Readers cannot recognize arrows in any context','Every part of an assembly must rotate together','Diagrams cannot show intermediate states','The arrow’s referent may be unclear.','purpose'),Q('Why may experts overlook a missing step?','Their prior knowledge allows them to fill the gap mentally','They cannot read instructions intended for novices','They always prefer words to pictures','They lack familiarity with the completed object','Expert knowledge can conceal an omission.','inference'),Q('What is one benefit of asking users to describe their intended action?','A misunderstanding can be identified before an incorrect action','The designer can eliminate all user testing','Every diagram can then be removed','The object no longer needs to be assembled','The third paragraph states this preventive role.'),Q('“Coordinate” most nearly means','make different elements work together','replace a complex element with a simpler one','assign a number to each component','repeat the same message without changes','The conclusion concerns combining verbal and visual information.','vocabulary')]},
{title:'A Clock in a Flower',text:`Many flowering plants open their flowers at characteristic times of day. This pattern may bring flowers into contact with pollinators when those animals are most active. It can also help protect delicate structures during less favorable conditions. The visible opening is the result of interacting biological and environmental processes.
Some plants respond directly to changes in light or temperature. Others also show an internal daily rhythm. Researchers can distinguish these influences by keeping plants under controlled conditions. If a pattern continues for a time without the usual daily changes in light, an internal timing mechanism is likely to contribute.
An internal rhythm is not entirely independent of the environment. Natural light cycles can synchronize it with local conditions. A plant moved into a different schedule may gradually shift its activity rather than changing instantly. The time required for adjustment gives researchers information about how environmental cues and internal processes interact.
These observations caution against interpreting a flower’s schedule as a simple reaction to the current weather. A flower opening on a cloudy morning may still reflect a rhythm established by earlier cycles. At the same time, internal timing does not make immediate conditions irrelevant. Both inherited timing systems and present conditions can influence when a flower is available to a visiting pollinator.`,qs:[Q('Why might opening at a characteristic time benefit a flowering plant?','It can coincide with the activity of pollinators','It ensures that every visiting animal pollinates it','It eliminates all effects of temperature','It prevents the flower from ever closing','The first paragraph identifies contact with active pollinators.'),Q('What observation supports the existence of an internal rhythm?','The pattern persists temporarily without normal daily light changes','The plant responds only when the room becomes warmer','The flowers open at a completely random time','The plant immediately loses all patterns in controlled conditions','Persistence under constant conditions suggests an internal component.','inference'),Q('What role can natural light cycles play?','They can synchronize an internal rhythm with the environment','They make biological timing mechanisms unnecessary','They prevent any gradual adjustment','They establish identical opening times for all species','The third paragraph describes environmental synchronization.'),Q('Why mention a cloudy morning?','To show why the current conditions alone may not explain opening','To establish that flowers open only under clouds','To show that earlier light cycles have no effect','To identify the main cause of pollinator decline','The example illustrates a rhythm influenced by prior cycles.','purpose'),Q('Which statement best summarizes the passage?','Flower-opening schedules can reflect both internal rhythms and environmental cues','Flower opening is determined entirely by the temperature at that moment','All plants use identical timing systems','Controlled experiments cannot separate biological and environmental influences','The text repeatedly develops the interaction of both factors.','main idea')]},
{title:'Choosing What a Map Leaves Out',text:`A map is useful partly because it leaves information out. A drawing that attempted to include every feature at its original size would be as difficult to navigate as the landscape itself. Mapmakers choose which relationships to emphasize according to the task the map is meant to support.
A transit diagram offers a familiar example. It may show stations at roughly equal intervals even when the actual distances between them differ greatly. The diagram helps passengers identify connections and the order of stops, but it may be unsuitable for estimating how far they must walk above ground. The distortion is purposeful rather than necessarily a mistake.
The same principle applies to thematic maps, which emphasize a particular kind of information. A map of rainfall can group measurements into shaded categories. The boundaries between categories simplify a continuous pattern, and the selected ranges influence how large differences appear. A reader should therefore examine the legend instead of treating every change in shading as an equally large change in the underlying measurements.
Digital maps can reveal additional layers on demand, yet they still involve selection. Screen size, scale and visual priority affect what is readily visible. Good map reading involves asking both what a map shows clearly and what its design makes difficult to infer.`,qs:[Q('What is the main argument?','A map’s simplifications should be understood in relation to its purpose','A map is useful only if every distance is represented precisely','Digital maps eliminate decisions about selection','Every distortion indicates careless mapmaking','Purpose explains selection and the limits of inference.','main idea'),Q('What does a transit diagram typically emphasize?','Connections and the sequence of stations','Precise walking distances above ground','The elevation of every street','Rainfall differences among neighborhoods','The second paragraph identifies the diagram’s main function.'),Q('Why does the author describe the distortion as purposeful?','It can improve the information needed for a particular task','It allows readers to ignore the intended use','It makes all measurements more accurate','It removes all differences between stations','A geographically distorted diagram may still serve passengers well.','purpose'),Q('Why should a reader examine the legend on a rainfall map?','Different shading categories may represent unequal numerical ranges','The legend always supplies unshown street names','Every shaded boundary is a physical barrier','Rainfall is measured only at category boundaries','The category ranges affect the apparent differences.','inference'),Q('“Thematic” describes a map that','focuses on a particular subject or type of information','has no selected scale','is drawn only on paper','shows every available feature equally','The passage defines thematic maps through their emphasis.','vocabulary')]},
];
